# Model provider for NPCs and the Game Master: mock (default, no key) | anthropic | openrouter | local
MODEL_PROVIDER=mock

# --- anthropic ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Optional custom endpoint or proxy (https, or http on localhost only). Do not include /v1.
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://my-proxy.example.com/anthropic

# --- openrouter (MODEL_PROVIDER=openrouter) ------------------------------------------------------
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...
# Optional; default https://openrouter.ai/api/v1 (must be https, except on localhost)
# OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1

# --- local OpenAI-compatible server (MODEL_PROVIDER=local) ---------------------------------------
# Ollama: http://localhost:11434/v1    LM Studio: http://localhost:1234/v1    (vLLM, llama.cpp server also work)
# Inside Docker, "localhost" is the container: use http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1 instead.
# LOCAL_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
# Optional: only if your server wants a token (a dummy value is fine for most). Sent as "Authorization: Bearer ..."
# LOCAL_API_KEY=
# For "local" NPC_MODEL and GM_MODEL are REQUIRED (model ids are server-specific), e.g. llama3.1 (Ollama).

# Model ids. Blank = the provider default: claude-sonnet-5-5 (anthropic), anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5 (openrouter).
# Prompts (scenario text, player lines) are sent to whichever endpoint you configure.
NPC_MODEL=
GM_MODEL=

RUNTIME_PORT=8080
# --- access and abuse limits (US-0017; see docs/THREAT_MODEL.md) ----------------------------------------------------------------
# FACILITATOR_TOKEN: when set, join_facilitator must present it. 16 to 256 printable ASCII characters, no spaces. Unset = the server is OPEN
# (anyone who can reach the port may join as facilitator) and prints a warning at startup. ./run.sh writes a random one into a NEW .env.
# The terminal client reads the same variable (or --token-file <path>, or a hidden prompt); there is no --token argument on purpose.
# An EMPTY value (FACILITATOR_TOKEN=) counts as unset, also when exported in the real environment, so it never overrides this file.
# Create one with: openssl rand -hex 24
# FACILITATOR_TOKEN=
# Failed player logins are counted per address AND role (US-0052): more than 5 wrong codes a minute for one role block that role from that
# address for a minute; other roles and the facilitator's handshake are not affected.
# Player join codes (US-0033) need no setting: the server issues one per player role for each new session and prints them ONCE at
# start (hashes only in data/sessions/<id>.codes.json; a restart keeps them). A player's client reads its code from JOIN_CODE, from
# --code-file <path> or a hidden prompt (there is no --code argument). Do not put a code in this file: it is per session and per person.
# RUNTIME_HOST: the interface to bind (default 0.0.0.0, every interface). Use 127.0.0.1 behind a TLS reverse proxy on the same machine.
# RUNTIME_HOST=0.0.0.0
# Connection caps (whole numbers). WS_MAX_CONNECTIONS: all clients together, 1 to 10000 (default 32). WS_MAX_CONNECTIONS_PER_IP: 1 to 10000
# (default 8); behind Docker Desktop, NAT or a proxy many people share one address, so raise it (or set TRUST_PROXY=1 behind a proxy you control).
# WS_MAX_CONNECTIONS=32
# WS_MAX_CONNECTIONS_PER_IP=8
# Per connection: messages per second WS_MSG_RATE 1 to 1000 (default 5) and the burst a client may spend at once WS_MSG_BURST 1 to 10000
# (default 20). A client that keeps exceeding them is closed. A connection that has not joined within WS_JOIN_TIMEOUT_MS (500 to 600000,
# default 10000) is closed.
# WS_MSG_RATE=5
# WS_MSG_BURST=20
# WS_JOIN_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS: comma separated browser origins that may connect (https://play.example.com). A handshake with any other Origin header is
# refused; the terminal client and the demo bots send none. Default: none allowed.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=
# TRUST_PROXY: 1 reads the client address from the last X-Forwarded-For entry (for the per-address limits). Only safe when clients cannot reach
# the server directly: bind RUNTIME_HOST=127.0.0.1 (Docker: publish 127.0.0.1:8080:8080). The server warns on a non-loopback host.
# TRUST_PROXY=0
# Behind the web client (apps/web) set TRUST_PROXY=1 and RUNTIME_HOST=127.0.0.1: the web server passes each browser's address this way.

# --- consent (US-0054; see docs/THREAT_MODEL.md, Consent notice versions) -------------------------------------------------------
# CONSENT_NOTICE_VERSION: the notice players must agree to before the start: consent/notice-<version>.md (default v2, which says a participant's seat may be played by an AI player; 1 to 32 characters of
# A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ -). The server does not start without the file. To change the notice add a new file (consent/notice-v3.md), set v3 and
# restart; keep the old files. Consents given before the start must be given again; a started session keeps the consents in its log.
# consent/notice-v3.md exists since US-0070 but is a DRAFT for legal and HR review (it describes voice recording): do not set v3 for real
# participants until it is signed off (docs/memory/backlog-and-decisions.md, go-live checklist).
# CONSENT_NOTICE_VERSION=v2
# NPC timeouts in milliseconds (whole numbers, 500 to 600000). Raise them for reasoning models, free-tier OpenRouter
# or slow local models. The reply deadline must be >= the first-token timeout.
# NPC_FIRST_TOKEN_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# NPC_REPLY_TIMEOUT_MS=20000
# Token budgets (max_tokens) per model call, whole numbers 50 to 4000. A reasoning model spends part of the budget thinking before it
# answers, and none of that thinking is shown, so the whole thinking phase must fit inside NPC_FIRST_TOKEN_TIMEOUT_MS. If the facilitator
# sees "empty reply: reasoning budget exhausted", raise these AND raise NPC_FIRST_TOKEN_TIMEOUT_MS and NPC_REPLY_TIMEOUT_MS with them
# (roughly the budget divided by the model's tokens per second, plus margin). A bigger budget alone makes the wait longer.
# Sampling temperature per model call (a decimal, 0 to 2). NPC_TEMPERATURE: AI characters (default 0.8); PLAYER_TEMPERATURE: the demo's
# generated player bots, `pnpm demo --showcase --live --players generated` (default 0.9); GM_TEMPERATURE: Game Master verdicts (default 0.2).
# NPC_TEMPERATURE=0.8
# PLAYER_TEMPERATURE=0.9
# GM_TEMPERATURE=0.2
# NPC_MAX_TOKENS=600
# GM_MAX_TOKENS=400
# Game Master reliability (US-0025). GM_TIMEOUT_MS: the deadline of one Game Master call with its retries and re-ask, 500 to 600000 (unset keeps
# max(NPC_REPLY_TIMEOUT_MS, 60 s); worst case 2 asks x (1 + MODEL_MAX_RETRIES) requests inside it). GM_REASK: 1 (default) asks once more after a reply with no usable verdict, 0 turns the re-ask off.
# GM_EVERY_N_UTTERANCES: judge the exit conditions (and each pending hidden-fact earned_when condition) after this many new utterances in a scene, 1 to 20 (default 3).
# GM_AUTO_RELEASE (US-0034): 0 (default) = when the Game Master judges a hidden fact's earned_when condition met it only SUGGESTS the release to the
# facilitator (/release <role> <n>). 1 = it releases the fact itself (recorded as a Game Master action; a warning is printed at startup). A participant
# who talks the model into it would then release a fact without the facilitator: leave it off unless you accept that.
# GM_TRANSCRIPT_WINDOW (US-0019): the least number of the current scene's latest utterances a Game Master prompt holds (default 40, 10 to 500, and at
# least GM_EVERY_N_UTTERANCES). It also holds every line since the last answered prompt for the same condition (up to 500, else an alert), the scene's
# first 2 lines and each AI character's last 2 lines with the line before each; runs left out are marked {"omitted": n}. A player's line pushed out by many later lines is no
# longer shown (a flooded objection can then allow a false exit): keep it generous.
# GM_TRACE_FILE: off by default; a file (relative to data/sessions; never a *.jsonl file there, a symbolic link or a hard-linked file) that records every raw Game Master reply and how it was read, mode 0600.
# It holds model replies about the whole conversation: keep it private. Replay it offline with `pnpm gm-eval --trace <file>`.
# GM_TIMEOUT_MS=
# GM_REASK=1
# GM_EVERY_N_UTTERANCES=3
# GM_AUTO_RELEASE=0
# GM_TRANSCRIPT_WINDOW=40
# GM_TRACE_FILE=
# Transient model errors (overloaded, rate limit, 5xx, connection reset) are retried before the NPC speaks its fallback line.
# MODEL_MAX_RETRIES: 0 to 5 (default 2, so up to 3 attempts; 0 disables). MODEL_RETRY_BASE_MS: 100 to 10000 (default 500; doubles per retry).
# Retries stop at the deadlines: the NPC timeouts above, and for the Game Master max(NPC_REPLY_TIMEOUT_MS, 60 s).
# A base above 4000 raises the backoff cap with it. A refused connection is retried too (a model server that is off costs ~1.5 s).
# MODEL_MAX_RETRIES=2
# MODEL_RETRY_BASE_MS=500
# SESSION_ID=local
# SESSION_START: resume (default) restores a session that was running when the server stopped, PAUSED (the facilitator types /resume);
# an ended session's log is moved aside. fresh always moves the old log aside and starts a new session (./run.sh --fresh).
# SESSION_START=resume
# SESSION_LOCK_STALE_MS: a session lock without a heartbeat for this long may be taken over by another server (10000 to 600000).
# SESSION_LOCK_STALE_MS=30000
# SCENARIO_DIR=scenarios/friday-escalation
DEPLOYMENT_STAGE=local
# Live model contract tests call the real API (cost!). Opt-in only: needs this flag AND ANTHROPIC_API_KEY.
# RUN_LIVE_MODEL_TESTS=1

# Post-session evaluator (`pnpm evaluate`, `pnpm demo --showcase --evaluate`; see docs/EVALUATOR.md). EVAL_MODEL defaults to NPC_MODEL.
# EVAL_MODEL=
# EVAL_MAX_TOKENS=3000
# EVAL_TEMPERATURE=0.2
# EVAL_TIMEOUT_MS=180000
# EVAL_TRANSCRIPT_CHARS=60000

# --- web client (apps/web, US-0056; see README "Players in a browser") ----------------------------------------------------
# These are read by the web app (next start / next dev), which does NOT read this root .env: export them in its environment or copy them
# into apps/web/.env.local (git-ignored). The runtime ignores them.
# RUNTIME_URL: the runtime WebSocket endpoint the web server connects to (ws:// or wss://, no credentials; never sent to the browser).
# RUNTIME_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:8080
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN: the exact origin players' browsers use (state-changing requests must send exactly this Origin). Over plain http the
# Secure cookie works only on localhost: anything else needs https behind a TLS reverse proxy that replaces X-Forwarded-For.
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000
# COOKIE_KEYS: participant cookie sealing keys, 1 to 8, newest first, as id:base64key (id 1 to 16 of A-Z a-z 0-9 _ -; 32 random bytes in
# padded base64). Create a key with: openssl rand -base64 32. Secret: never commit a real one. Rotate by adding a new key first; dropping
# a key signs out the players whose cookies it sealed (in slice 2a they cannot rejoin).
# COOKIE_KEYS=k1:REPLACE_WITH_BASE64_32_BYTES
# COOKIE_MAX_AGE_HOURS: lifetime of the participant cookie in hours, 1 to 168 (default 12).
# COOKIE_MAX_AGE_HOURS=12
# Live event stream caps (whole numbers): in total 1 to 10000 (default 200), per session 1 to 1000 (default 64), per client address
# 1 to 1000 (default 16; players behind one office address share it). Set the runtime's WS_MAX_CONNECTIONS to at least the total plus
# the facilitators.
# WEB_MAX_STREAMS=200
# WEB_MAX_STREAMS_PER_SESSION=64
# WEB_MAX_STREAMS_PER_ADDRESS=16
# LIVEKIT_URL (US-0071, voice A1b; optional): the LiveKit server's PUBLIC origin the participants' browsers connect to: wss://<host>[:port]
# (the pilot), or ws://127.0.0.1:<port> / ws://[::1]:<port> with an explicit port for local development (a LAN ws:// is refused). No path,
# query or credentials. Checked even with VOICE_ENABLED=0: an invalid value fails the web app's config load for EVERY API route. Without it
# the session page offers no voice and POST /api/v1/voice/token answers 503. A valid value makes /session/<id> (only) allow the microphone
# (microphone=(self)) and this origin (plus its https twin) in connect-src, whatever VOICE_ENABLED says: leave it unset unless voice is on. The web app
# never gets a LiveKit key or secret (those are the control service's, "voice input" below).
# LIVEKIT_URL=wss://livekit.example.test

# --- facilitator console (apps/web, US-0057; read by the web app like the settings above) ---------------------------------------------
# The console at /console is DISABLED (it says "The console is not set up on this server.", and the web server logs which of these three
# are unset) unless all three are set. A player-only deployment sets none of them.
# FACILITATOR_SECRET: the shared secret a facilitator types to sign in, at least 16 characters. Secret: never commit a real one. Changing
# it signs every facilitator out. Create one with: openssl rand -base64 24
# FACILITATOR_SECRET=
# LAUNCHER_URL: where the web server reaches the control service (http://, never sent to the browser). Setting it switches the web server to
# launcher mode: every player join and stream then looks up the session's own runtime through the control service, so LAUNCHER_TOKEN is
# required for player routes too (without it the web server refuses to start), and RUNTIME_URL is not used. Leave it unset to keep the
# single-runtime mode (RUNTIME_URL) exactly as before.
# LAUNCHER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787
# LAUNCHER_TOKEN: the token the web server presents to the control service (and the control service requires), at least 32 characters.
# Secret. Create one with: openssl rand -hex 32
# LAUNCHER_TOKEN=

# CONTROL_MANAGED, NO_DOTENV and SESSION_DATA_DIR are set by the control service on the runtimes it starts. SESSION_DATA_DIR may also be
# set by hand to continue a session from the terminal (docs/ROLLBACK.md); the other two are not for users.

# --- control service (services/control, US-0058; `pnpm --filter @acr/control start`) ---------------------------------------------------
# A small loopback-only HTTP service the web server calls to create sessions: it starts one runtime per session and keeps their codes in
# memory only. It reads this root .env (the real environment wins). Provider keys and NPC/Game Master settings above are passed on to the
# runtimes it starts through an allowlist; nothing else is.
# LAUNCHER_TOKEN (above) is required here and must be the same value the web server uses; at least 32 characters.
# CONTROL_PORT: the port it listens on, on 127.0.0.1 only (default 8787; point the web server's LAUNCHER_URL at it).
# CONTROL_PORT=8787
# CONTROL_DATA_DIR: REQUIRED, an absolute directory for the session list (sessions.json), the sessions' data and the child logs.
# CONTROL_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/scenecoach/control
# MAX_SESSIONS: non-ended sessions allowed at once, 1 to 100 (default 10). A stopped session still counts until it is marked ended: there is
# no end or remove action before slice 2b-2 (operator recovery: README section 5 and docs/ROLLBACK.md).
# MAX_SESSIONS=10
# SCENARIOS_DIR: absolute path of the scenarios folder offered to facilitators (default: scenarios/ in this repository).
# SCENARIOS_DIR=
# CONTROL_LOG_DIR: absolute directory for each runtime's stdout/stderr log (default: <CONTROL_DATA_DIR>/child-logs). Mode 0700; files 0600.
# CONTROL_LOG_DIR=
# CONTROL_GRACE_MS: how long a stopping runtime may take before it is force-killed, 0 to 3600000 (default 30000).
# CONTROL_GRACE_MS=30000
# CONTROL_READY_TIMEOUT_MS: how long a starting runtime may take to report ready, 1000 to 600000 (default 30000).
# CONTROL_READY_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# The web server waits 120 s for a create, restart or whole-session reissue, so keep CONTROL_READY_TIMEOUT_MS + 2 * CONTROL_GRACE_MS below
# that (the control service warns at startup when the sum exceeds 110000 ms).
# REPORT_ACCESS_DAYS: how long a participant's report link works, a whole number of days from 1 to 365 (default 90). A facilitator can revoke it
# sooner. Report links read the released reports in <CONTROL_DATA_DIR>/reports (see README: evaluate with --out there).
# The control service also WRITES to <CONTROL_DATA_DIR>/reports (participants' self-ratings and responses): run it as the same user as `pnpm moderate`,
# or make that directory group-writable by both, and do not mount it read-only (README, Settings).
# REPORT_ACCESS_DAYS=90
# REPORT_EVALUATE_MARGIN_MS: extra time for one evaluate run started from the console, 0 to 3600000 ms (default 120000). The run is stopped
# after (the most model calls it can make) x (one call's timeout) + this margin, where one call's timeout is the larger of EVAL_TIMEOUT_MS and
# NPC_REPLY_TIMEOUT_MS, each clamped to 500..600000 ms, as the evaluator computes it. The control service runs the evaluator with the model
# settings above and NO_DOTENV=1, writing into <CONTROL_DATA_DIR>/reports.
# REPORT_EVALUATE_MARGIN_MS=120000

# --- live AI players (services/control, US-0060, slice 2b-3b; the control service reads these at startup) ---
# An AI player fills a player role until the start. The control service runs its bot: it calls the model with the keys and NPC settings above
# (NPC_FIRST_TOKEN_TIMEOUT_MS, NPC_REPLY_TIMEOUT_MS, NPC_MAX_TOKENS; one call never takes longer than NPC_REPLY_TIMEOUT_MS + 5 s). An invalid
# value stops the control service at startup with a message that names the variable, never the value. PLAYER_TEMPERATURE (above) applies too.
# PLAYER_MODEL: the model id for live AI players (default: NPC_MODEL).
# PLAYER_MODEL=
# AI_PLAYER_MIN_GAP_MS: least time between two lines of one AI player, 0 to 600000 (default 15000).
# AI_PLAYER_MIN_GAP_MS=15000
# AI_PLAYER_NUDGE_MS: quiet time before an AI player speaks unprompted (only while a human is connected), 10000 to 3600000 (default 90000).
# AI_PLAYER_NUDGE_MS=90000
# AI_PLAYER_LINES_PER_SCENE: most lines one AI player speaks in a scene, 1 to 100 (default 10).
# AI_PLAYER_LINES_PER_SCENE=10
# AI_PLAYER_SETTLE_MS: wait after the last line before an AI player replies, 0 to 60000 (default 6000).
# AI_PLAYER_SETTLE_MS=6000
# AI_PLAYER_CALLS_PER_SESSION: model calls all AI players of one session share, 0 to 1000 (default 60). At 0 they never speak.
# AI_PLAYER_CALLS_PER_SESSION=60

# --- voice input, slices A1a and A1b (EPIC-0003, US-0070, US-0071; OFF by default and not for participants yet) ------------------------------
# Nothing here is needed for a text-only session. There is no audio retention yet (slice A2): voice must not be offered to outside
# participants before it. With voice on, the speech worker reads WAV files (file ingest, `pnpm demo --voice`) or, with SPEECH_INGEST=livekit
# and the LiveKit settings below, the seat rooms of a LiveKit server (the web app's LIVEKIT_URL, "web client" above, is then needed too).
# An invalid value names the variable, never the value.
# Runtime settings (copied to each control-managed runtime through CHILD_ENV_ALLOWLIST, services/control/src/child-env.ts):
# VOICE_ENABLED: exactly 0 or 1 (default 0; "true" or "on" is refused). With 1 the runtime refuses to start unless the control service started it (CONTROL_MANAGED=1) and
# CONSENT_NOTICE_VERSION is v3 or later; a terminal runtime (`pnpm dev`) with VOICE_ENABLED=1 does not start.
# VOICE_ENABLED=0
# TURN_END_MS: how long after a voice turn's last speech frame the AI characters, the Game Master and the AI players react, a whole number of
# milliseconds from 300 to 3000 (default 800). Read only with VOICE_ENABLED=1 (a stray value never stops a text-only session).
# TURN_END_MS=800
# LiveKit (US-0071, A1b; read by the control service ONLY: no runtime and no speech worker ever gets one of these; the worker gets a
# fresh 10-minute token per seat room over IPC). Without the key and the secret LiveKit is off (no voice token is issued). Both or neither.
# These are checked whenever they are set, even with VOICE_ENABLED=0: a bad LiveKit setting stops the control service's start.
# Voice tokens are issued only with SPEECH_INGEST=livekit as well (with file ingest nobody listens to a seat room).
# LIVEKIT_API_KEY: the LiveKit API key (printable, no space). Never `--dev` keys.
# LIVEKIT_API_KEY=
# LIVEKIT_API_SECRET: the LiveKit API secret, at least 32 characters. A secret: never commit or log it.
# LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=
# LIVEKIT_HOST: the LiveKit server API on THIS machine, http://127.0.0.1:<port> or http://[::1]:<port> (default http://127.0.0.1:7880);
# control removes stale seat identities and deletes the seat rooms through it, and the worker signals on ws:// to the same host and port.
# LIVEKIT_HOST=http://127.0.0.1:7880
# US-0072: control also probes LIVEKIT_HOST (GET /, 2 s) when a speech worker exits because it lost LiveKit: no answer is an outage (not
# counted toward the five restarts; probed again 2, 4, 8, 16 s after the previous answer, then every 30 s), any answer means the exit
# counts as a fault.
# VOICE_TIMING_LOG: exactly 0 or 1 (default 0; anything else stops the control service). With 1, control appends one line per recorded
# voice line to <CONTROL_LOG_DIR>/voice-timing.log (0600, at most 1 MiB): the session id and two millisecond counts, nothing else:
# end_to_append_ms runs from the voice-detector end of the segment (includes the hangover, VOICE_HANGOVER_MS), start_to_append_ms from
# the segment's first frame. Delete or move voice-timing.log before each room test (lines are appended, never rotated).
# Read it with `pnpm voice:timings <file>` (the room test). Leave it at 0 outside a measurement.
# VOICE_TIMING_LOG=0
# Speech worker settings (the worker is a child of the control service; it gets ONLY these through SPEECH_ENV_ALLOWLIST,
# services/control/src/voice/speech-env.ts, plus PATH, NODE_* and SPEECH_INGEST_ROLES, which control sets from the session's player roles):
# VOICE_HANGOVER_MS: silence that closes a speech segment, 300 to 600 ms (default 500).
# VOICE_HANGOVER_MS=500
# VOICE_MAX_SEGMENT_MS: a segment is split at its quietest point once it reaches this length, 2000 to 6000 ms (default 2500).
# VOICE_MAX_SEGMENT_MS=2500
# STT_PROVIDER: whisper (default: a local whisper.cpp whisper-server over HTTP) or fake (the scripted speech-to-text of the demo and tests).
# STT_PROVIDER=whisper
# STT_URL: required with STT_PROVIDER=whisper. LOOPBACK ONLY: http://127.0.0.1:<port>[/<path>] or http://[::1]:<port>[/<path>] with an
# explicit port and no user, query or fragment (`localhost`, a LAN address and https are refused). whisper-server has no authentication.
# STT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8178
# STT_COLD_TIMEOUT_MS: deadline of a "cold" call (the first call, and the first after STT_IDLE_COLD_MS without a call), 1000 to 300000 ms
# (default 30000), so a local model that is loading is not reported as a failure. Every other call has the 5 s segment deadline.
# STT_COLD_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# STT_IDLE_COLD_MS: idle time after which the next call counts as cold, 1000 to 86400000 ms (default 600000).
# STT_IDLE_COLD_MS=600000
# STT_MODEL: optional; checked at the worker's start (1 to 64 characters, dot-separated parts of letters, digits, _ or -, e.g. small.en
# or large-v3-turbo-q5_0); a label only: the model file `pnpm voice:up` starts is STT_MODEL_FILE (below).
# STT_MODEL=
# STT_FAKE_SCRIPT: required with STT_PROVIDER=fake: the ABSOLUTE path of a JSON file {"<roleId>": ["line 1", ...]}; each call is appended
# to <file>.calls.jsonl (role and call number only, never text).
# STT_FAKE_SCRIPT=/absolute/path/to/fake-script.json
# SPEECH_INGEST: file (default: WAV files, the demo and CI) or livekit (US-0071: the worker joins each recording seat's LiveKit room with a
# fresh token from control and listens only to the current holder's microphone track). livekit needs LIVEKIT_API_KEY and LIVEKIT_API_SECRET
# (the control service refuses to start without them) and a glibc host (the native LiveKit client has no Alpine/musl build).
# SPEECH_INGEST=file
# SPEECH_INGEST_DIR: required whenever a file-ingest worker starts: an ABSOLUTE directory holding <roleId>.wav per player role (16 kHz mono 16-bit PCM,
# at most 100 MiB each; a missing file means no audio for that role). With a missing or invalid worker setting the worker exits at once
# (fixed error lines in <CONTROL_LOG_DIR>/<session>.speech.log); control restarts it after 0.5, 1, 2, 4 and 8 s, and after five failed
# restarts in a row voice is off for that session with one alert (US-0071). The session keeps running by text throughout. An alert raised in
# the lobby is shown to the facilitator when the session starts.
# SPEECH_INGEST_DIR=/absolute/path/to/wav-folder

# ---- Voice operations (US-0072, slice A1c): `pnpm voice:up`, `voice:check`, `voice:down`, `voice:timings` (docs/VOICE_RUNBOOK.md) ----
# Read by services/voice-ops only (from this .env, the real environment winning, as control reads it). voice:up never sets VOICE_ENABLED,
# CONSENT_NOTICE_VERSION or SPEECH_INGEST. It ADDS, only when absent and never printed: LIVEKIT_API_KEY, LIVEKIT_API_SECRET and
# STT_URL (http://127.0.0.1:<port>/<32 hex>); do not export these three in your shell (voice:up refuses). It needs this .env to exist
# already (./run.sh), mode 0600, not tracked by git.
# VOICE_ACCESS: local (default: one machine, http://localhost:3000, no proxy) or lan (a hostname, a certificate and the Caddy proxy).
# VOICE_ACCESS=local
# VOICE_STATE_DIR: absolute, outside the repository (default <HOME>/.scenecoach/voice; 0700): state.json, lock, livekit.env, whisper.log.
# VOICE_STATE_DIR=/absolute/path/outside/the/repo
# STT_MODEL_FILE: absolute path of the whisper.cpp ggml model whisper-server loads (default <HOME>/.scenecoach/models/ggml-<STT_MODEL>.bin).
# voice:up never downloads a model.
# STT_MODEL_FILE=/absolute/path/to/ggml-small.en.bin
# VOICE_WEB_PORT: the web app's loopback port, 1024 to 65535 (default 3000).
# VOICE_WEB_PORT=3000
# lan mode only:
# VOICE_HOSTNAME: the pilot hostname (a DNS name of two or more labels resolving to this laptop's private address).
# VOICE_HOSTNAME=<hostname>
# VOICE_NODE_IP: this laptop's private IPv4 address (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16); default: found from the network interfaces.
# VOICE_NODE_IP=
# VOICE_HTTPS_PORT (default 443) and VOICE_LIVEKIT_TLS_PORT (default 7443): the proxy's two sites; they must differ.
# VOICE_HTTPS_PORT=443
# VOICE_LIVEKIT_TLS_PORT=7443
# VOICE_TLS_MODE: files (default, the only path in A1c). dns is refused: no DNS provider has been chosen (plan Task 13 skipped). With
# dns and no valid VOICE_DNS_PROVIDER the settings check exits 2 ("VOICE_DNS_PROVIDER is required with VOICE_TLS_MODE=dns ..."); only
# with a valid provider name does voice:up say "VOICE_TLS_MODE=dns is not available yet ...". No DNS token is used.
# VOICE_TLS_MODE=files
# VOICE_TLS_CERT_FILE and VOICE_TLS_KEY_FILE: absolute paths of the owner-supplied certificate and key (chmod 600 the key).
# VOICE_TLS_CERT_FILE=/absolute/path/to/cert.pem
# VOICE_TLS_KEY_FILE=/absolute/path/to/key.pem
# AUDIO_MIN_FREE_MB (512 to 1000000, default 2048) and AUDIO_DIR (absolute; default <CONTROL_DATA_DIR>/audio): read by voice:check's
# disk and backup checks, and (US-0073) by the audio store: control and the speech worker stop recording below the free-space floor.
# AUDIO_MIN_FREE_MB=2048
# AUDIO_DIR=/absolute/path/to/audio
# AUDIO_MAX_RETENTION_DAYS (US-0073): recordings older than this are deleted by control's sweep, 1 to 30 (default 30). A whole number;
# anything else stops control at start.
# AUDIO_MAX_RETENTION_DAYS=30
